comments[6]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-04T11:25:06.351Z","harness:claude-code","Code evidence, verified on feat/sdk-caller-contract (2026.11.4 source). The description's pointer is imprecise: src/core/store/settings.ts buildTelemetrySettings only normalizes whichever file it is given. The global-only behaviour comes from src/core/telemetry/runtime.ts. Every delivery site calls readSettings(globalPmRoot), including ensureInstallationId, which returns the endpoint the queue flush uses. No site reads the project settings, so the project telemetry block that pm init writes is never consulted for the endpoint. Fix direction: one env override that resolves at that seam and is reported in env_overrides, and pm init stops writing the inert project block or documents it as ignored."
  "2026-11-04T13:58:06.145Z","harness:claude-code","Consent defect reproduced on the 2026.11.4 build, which has the same root cause as the endpoint report. The setup wizard asks 'Enable telemetry for this project? [Y/n]' and writes the answer only into the project .agents/pm/settings.json (sdk/init.ts createNewInitSettings sets settings.telemetry.enabled and first_run_prompt_completed, then calls writeSettings(params.pmRoot, ...)). The telemetry runtime reads consent only from the global profile, so the answer has no effect. Reproduction in a disposable HOME and PM_GLOBAL_PATH: drive the wizard through a pseudo-terminal, accept the prefix and preset defaults, and answer n. The project file then holds telemetry.enabled=false, while pm ops telemetry status reports enabled=true and configured_enabled=true. Running pm list and pm ops telemetry flush afterwards delivered the queued events: the disposable profile's runtime/telemetry/state.json recorded last_successful_flush_at with queue_entries=0 against the production endpoint. A user who declines telemetry in the wizard therefore keeps sending it. This relates to the closed revocation work in pm-yjq2x0, which made a global revocation authoritative but never connected the wizard's answer to the global profile. Fix direction: the wizard writes its consent to the global profile, or asks nothing, and the project telemetry block is no longer written."
  "2026-11-04T14:15:10.282Z","harness:claude-code","Third surface, reproduced on the 2026.11.4 build in a disposable profile: pm config project set telemetry-tracking --policy disabled returns changed=true and writes telemetry.enabled=false into the project settings.json, but pm ops telemetry status still reports configured_enabled=true, because consent is read only from the global profile. All three surfaces (init wizard answer, project telemetry-tracking, project telemetry.endpoint) come from one fact: the project telemetry block is written and accepted but never read."
  "2026-11-04T14:18:04.937Z","harness:claude-code","Fourth surface, the most severe. docs/CONFIGURATION.md tells users to opt out persistently with 'pm config set telemetry-tracking off'. The scope-less shorthand resolves to project scope (src/cli/register-setup.ts resolveConfigPositionals: resolvedScope 'project'). Run inside a project on the 2026.11.4 build, it returns scope=project, policy=disabled, changed=true and writes the project file, while pm ops telemetry status keeps configured_enabled=true. The documented opt-out therefore does not opt out for anyone who runs it inside a tracker, which is the normal place to run pm. Fix: the scope-less shorthand for telemetry-tracking resolves to the global profile, and an explicit project scope refuses with the global command as remedy."
  "2026-11-04T18:35:41.280Z","harness:claude-code","PR 1661 review follow-up (CodeRabbit, 4 findings in this item's scope) fixed on the same branch. (1) The init wizard re-checks consent after its prompt. If another process recorded a choice while the prompt was open, that choice stands and init warns telemetry_consent_kept:recorded_while_prompting. The write reconciles against that re-read under the settings lock, and an unreadable global profile is never asked about, matching the runtime first-use prompt. (2) The init result, its concise summary and its display line report the global profile's telemetry block, the one the runtime reads, instead of the project default enabled=true. (3) pm health --check-telemetry sends URL credentials of the endpoint (PM_TELEMETRY_ENDPOINT or configured) as a Basic authorization header, because fetch refuses a URL with userinfo while event delivery through http.request accepts one. (4) A malformed shared state.json now raises telemetry_state_invalid_json even when an endpoint override reads its own state file. Specs: init-command (3 new cases), health-command (2 new cases), all passing. The remaining missing-file race, where a write from a read that found no settings.json replaces a file created meanwhile, is reproduced and filed as pm-nroemi.\n"
  "2026-11-04T19:11:07.206Z","harness:claude-code","PR 1661, independent review agent round on the CodeRabbit fixes (8 findings, all addressed on the branch). (1) P2: the init result had started to carry the whole global telemetry block, so pm init --json and --verbose printed the global endpoint with any URL credentials and the installation id. The result now takes only enabled, first_run_prompt_completed and capture_level from the global profile, and a spec asserts that neither secret appears. (2) The consent re-check is now atomic. WriteSettingsOptions gains skipIfStored, a compare-and-set consulted under the settings lock with the stored document. The wizard write skips when a choice is already stored, which also covers a profile created by another process after the re-read, and warns telemetry_consent_kept:recorded_while_prompting. A profile that became unreadable during the prompt is left alone, with the warning telemetry_consent_not_recorded:global_settings_unreadable. When init targets the global profile itself, the create write still records the answer, and that missing-file case stays with pm-nroemi. (3) The health probe decodes userinfo with a fallback for malformed escapes, always strips it from the probe URL, and redacts the probe URL and any URL credentials from transport error messages. redactTelemetryEndpoint now also strips credentials, query and fragment from text that is not a valid URL, through the new shared stripUrlCredentials. The SDK surface acknowledges the WriteSettingsOptions signature change.\n"
notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-04T13:29:57.244Z","harness:claude-code","Design constraints found while scoping (source of 2026.11.4). (1) Endpoint consumers: src/core/telemetry/runtime.ts ensureInstallationId (feeds every queue flush and the detached flush worker), src/sdk/telemetry.ts buildTelemetryStatusSummary (status.endpoint), and two health probes in src/sdk/governance/health.ts. An override must resolve through one helper used by all of them, or status will disagree with delivery. (2) Queued events (QueuedTelemetryEvent) do not record an endpoint, and the queue is per global profile. Suppose a test sets an override and shares the real global profile, and delivery to the local collector fails. Its events stay queued, and the next ordinary run flushes them to production. The override must either stamp the endpoint on each queued entry and flush per entry, or the docs must require a disposable PM_GLOBAL_PATH beside it. Pending OTel spans already carry a per-span endpoint. (3) An invalid override must fail closed, disabling delivery and reporting it in env_overrides, never fall back to the configured endpoint. A fallback reproduces exactly the incident: the author believes delivery is local while it reaches production. (4) The project telemetry block must not be honored. A cloned repository would otherwise redirect a user's telemetry to an endpoint the repository chose. So the second acceptance criterion resolves as 'no longer written by pm init'. serializeSettings writes the full PmSettings, including telemetry, for every root, so the omission belongs in serialization for project roots, with init fixtures updated."
